# How-To — jq Query to Live WordPress Page

The full workflow, start to finish, using the `recurring` events page as
the worked example. Same pattern applies to every page in this build.

## Step 1 — Confirm what data you're working with

Get a live, complete snapshot of every event, all fields, from the
database:
```bash
bash events-schema-cli.sh
```
This calls `oswp_get_event_record()` (the shared helper in
`functions.php`) against every event post, live, no caching. Treat this
as your reference — if a field doesn't appear here, it doesn't exist for
PHP to use either.

## Step 2 — Design the filter as a jq query, benchtest against real data

Before writing any PHP, express the selection logic as jq, run it, and
eyeball the results:
```bash
bash events-schema-cli.sh | jq '[.[] | select(.is_recurring == true) | {id, title, event_date, event_end, recurrence_day, recurrence_frequency, entry_time}]'
```
Check: does this select the right events? Are the right fields present?
Fix the filter here, cheaply, before any PHP exists. This step catches
scope mistakes early — much cheaper to fix a jq filter than debug a
half-built page template.

## Step 3 — Benchtest the actual transformation logic via `wp eval`

If the page needs more than raw field values (e.g. building a formatted
display string, date reformatting), prototype that logic directly in
PHP via `wp eval` — same WordPress environment your real template will
run in, but no file, no page, immediate terminal output:
```bash
docker exec -i oswp-wordpress-1 wp eval '
$events = new WP_Query([
    "post_type"      => "event",
    "posts_per_page" => -1,
]);

while ( $events->have_posts() ): $events->the_post();
    $record = oswp_get_event_record( get_the_ID() );
    if ( empty( $record["is_recurring"] ) ) {
        continue;
    }
    // ...build and print whatever the display logic needs...
endwhile;
wp_reset_postdata();
' --allow-root
```
Iterate here until the output is correct. This step caught two real bugs
in this build: the `strtotime()` date-parsing bug (US month/day
ambiguity silently swapping day and month — fixed with
`DateTime::createFromFormat()` using the known source format instead),
and confirmed `entry_time`'s correct display wording ("Doors open...")
before committing it to a page.

**Do not skip this step for anything involving date parsing, string
formatting, or conditional logic** — it's much faster to fix a wrong
answer in a one-off terminal command than in a full page template.

## Step 4 — Convert the confirmed logic into a real page template file

Once Step 3's output is correct, wrap the same PHP in HTML and write it
to the theme, using `docker exec` with a heredoc:
```bash
docker exec -i oswp-wordpress-1 bash -c "cat > /var/www/html/wp-content/themes/oswp-child/page-{slug}.php" << 'PHP_EOF'
<?php
get_header();
// ...same logic proven in Step 3, now wrapped in HTML...
get_footer();
PHP_EOF
```

**Critical: this whole block must be RUN as a command in your terminal
(paste and press enter), not saved into a file yourself.** A recurring
mistake in this build: pasting the command into a text editor and saving
it under the target filename produces a file containing the *command
text itself* (including the `docker exec ... << 'PHP_EOF'` wrapper),
not the PHP payload — invalid, unusable. The `docker exec` command has
to execute; only its *effect* (the file landing inside the container)
is what you want.

## Step 5 — Fix host-side file permissions (if you want to hand-edit later)

Files created via `docker exec` are owned by the container's user
(`root`/`www-data`), not your host user — you can view them, but vim
will show them read-only until fixed:
```bash
sudo chown simon:simon child/page-{slug}.php
```

## Step 6 — Validate syntax before touching the browser

```bash
docker exec -i oswp-wordpress-1 php -l /var/www/html/wp-content/themes/oswp-child/page-{slug}.php
```
Must say "No syntax errors detected" before proceeding — catches typos
for free, before wasting a browser reload on a blank/broken page.

## Step 7 — Register the page (create the database record)

The file alone does nothing — WordPress only uses it once a matching
**page** exists in the database with the same slug:
```bash
docker exec -i oswp-wordpress-1 wp post list --post_type=page --name={slug} --allow-root
```
If empty, create it:
```bash
docker exec -i oswp-wordpress-1 wp post create --post_type=page --post_title="Display Title" --post_name={slug} --post_status=publish --allow-root
```

## Step 8 — Load and confirm

```
http://localhost:8080/{slug}/
```
Compare against Step 2/3's confirmed-correct output — should match.

## The whole chain, summarised

```
events-schema-cli.sh          (raw data, live)
        |
   jq filter                  (design + benchtest the selection)
        |
   wp eval                    (design + benchtest the display logic)
        |
   docker exec heredoc        (commit proven logic to a real file)
        |
   php -l                     (syntax check)
        |
   wp post create              (register the page)
        |
   browser                    (final confirmation)
```

Each step is cheap to fix in isolation. The expensive mistake is
skipping straight to Step 4 and debugging logic errors inside a full
page template rather than catching them earlier, where they're a
one-line jq or `wp eval` fix.
